--embed-metadata

# Using `yt-dlp` to access YouTube through a proxy may cause it to be flagged as
# a bot, which in turn forces you to sign in. The --cookies-from-browser option
# allows `yt-dlp` to grab the cookies from a browser where you are signed in to
# YouTube to complete authentication.
#
# The n-parameter (or simply n) challenge, a specialized security mechanism that
# YouTube uses to prevent unauthorized video downloads and perform bot detection
# on the client side, is delivered to the web client in the form of JavaScript.
# The client must solve the n-parameter challenge specific to a YouTube video to
# obtain permission to access it. `yt-dlp` requires an external JavaScript
# runtime to execute its EJS component to solve the challenge. If you are using
# a JavaScript runtime other than Deno, you must supply the --js-runtime option
# to `yt-dlp` to specify the JavaScript runtime to use.
#
# See `man yt-dlp` for all supported web browsers and JavaScript runtimes.
#
# References:
# - https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/wiki/Extractors#youtube
# - https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/wiki/PO-Token-Guide
# - https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/wiki/EJS
--js-runtimes=node

